{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And all your servants shall come down, etc.</b> Here he promised that these servants of his would live and not one of them would die in the plague of the firstborn. Or perhaps they were all non-firstborn and there was no firstborn among them, and he knew this through prophetic knowledge. Or even if there were firstborn among them, nonetheless he excluded them from the category of the plague, and the reason was not on account of their righteousness but rather because the wicked Pharaoh had humiliated him before them and spoken to him like a base fellow — ״See my face no more, etc., for in the day, etc.״ — therefore he too needed to be humiliated before them, according to the rule that one who shames his fellow in public must appease him in public. And you will find that Moses was particular about this matter, as it is written: ״וַיֵּצֵא מֵעִם פַּרְעֹה בָּחֳרִי אָף.״ And note: even though Moses said only ״and all your servants shall come down,״ he intended to say even about him, that he himself would arise in the night, but he spoke in a manner of honor." "<b>And they shall bow down to me, to say.</b> The reason he did not say explicitly ״and they will say to me״ is that he intended to say that the act of bowing down itself would convey that their intent was to say ״Get you out,״ since he is master over them — if so, the master does his will and there is no authority over him." "It further intends that, since the custom of kings is that when a person comes to speak with them about a matter, he bows down before them, and from this the king knows that they wish to speak with him — for it is not proper conduct to speak before a great king without asking permission, and it is not the way of honor to request permission verbally, therefore one bows down, and by this the king knows that he desires to say something, and he says to him, Speak. And this is what is said: <b>and they shall bow down to me, to say</b> — meaning, that the bowing down is to signal that they wish to speak, and the speaking is ״Get you out, etc.״ And even though Scripture did not mention this detail on that night, it abbreviated and stated something greater than what is said here: that Pharaoh himself arose in the night, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:26:49.026453562Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}